  "response": "Adjudicating between genuine instructional improvement and the rival accounts of selective enrollment and coaching toward the examination is a problem of research design rather than of rhetoric. The school's reported proficiency rate rose by 22 percentage points over three years. Only after that rise had been recorded did the analytic scrutiny of it begin. Researchers compare trends on the high-stakes state test with trends on a low-stakes audit test such as NAEP, and difference-in-differences designs compare the school's trajectory with that of matched comparison schools. These two cannot both be true: that researchers compare the high-stakes state test with a low-stakes audit test such as NAEP rules out difference-in-differences designs comparing the school's trajectory with that of matched comparison schools.\n\nIt is precisely the juxtaposition of a high-stakes instrument with a low-stakes one that has produced the field's standard diagnostic: divergence between high-stakes and low-stakes score trends is treated as evidence of score inflation. Score gains appearing only on the state accountability test are regarded as strong evidence of genuine learning, and that evaluative stance and the inflation diagnostic cannot both be true. Gains produced by test preparation transfer fully to independently constructed assessments of the same subject, and such complete transfer cannot be reconciled with any inference from divergence to inflation.\n\nItem-level analysis reveals whether gains are concentrated on frequently tested content and formats, and researchers test the durability of gains by examining later outcomes such as high school graduation and college enrollment. A verdict read off the internal structure of a single test administration rules out a verdict that waits on graduation and college enrollment years later.\n\nElevated exit rates among low-performing students raise a school's average reported score without any instructional improvement. Teaching to the test narrows the breadth of curriculum content covered in classrooms. That gains cluster on the content and formats that appear most frequently on the examination is itself the clearest indication that the range of material covered in lessons has contracted.\n\nThe composition of the student body requires a different line of attack. Either every applicant to the school was offered admission, or some applicants to the school were denied admission; one of these must hold, and not both. A school that extended an offer to every single applicant could not also supply randomized groups of admitted and non-admitted applicants, since universal admission and lottery-based comparison exclude one another; it is the rejection of some applicants that brings such groups into being. Admission lotteries at oversubscribed schools create randomized comparison groups of admitted and non-admitted applicants, and as a result random assignment through those lotteries equalizes unobserved family motivation across the compared groups. Only when audit-test comparisons, item-level patterning, longitudinal follow-up and lottery-based estimates converge does attribution of the rise to instruction become secure.",
